{
  "comment": "The operation sequence both drives run, in order, on copies of the same start deck. params is the nested Rails params hash; each drive encodes it the same way (bracketed form keys, or multipart when a step uploads). upload values name a file under fixtures/.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "name": "get-home",
      "mod": "core",
      "method": "get",
      "path": "/",
      "deliberate": {
        "why": "Ruby names each script bundle by its wall clock, and the battery's fixture clock names each write by its own step, so the two engines' .js entries differ in count. The output card has no history (card-mod-assets set/right/asset_output.rb:9-11), so its file takes the stand-in action id Time.now.to_i (card-mod-carrierwave lib/carrier_wave/file_card_uploader.rb:147-151, 202-205, 225-226), and store_output writes it on each update (card-mod-assets set/abstract/asset_outputter.rb:36-51). The first request's Card::Assets.refresh (:cautious, card-mod-assets lib/card/assets.rb:13-27, 86-91) re-stores the 15 mod script inputs one at a time, and each re-store rewrites the bundle (card-mod-assets set/abstract/asset_inputter.rb update_referers_after_input_changed). The committed deck holds those inputs unminified (it was built under the development environment), and the refresh stores each one minified (compress_assets true, card-mod-script set/abstract/00_script.rb:18-27), so each of the 15 writes carries different bytes and the last is the fully minified bundle. The port names the bundle by the same rule, floor(clock / 1000) (src/card/actions/style-asset-events.ts, the stamp in updateAssetOutput); the battery drive gives it a fixture clock that steps one second per read (tools/parity-side-effects/ts-drive.mjs), so its 15 writes keep 15 names. Ruby's 15 writes land on 13 names: two land in a second an earlier write already took and overwrite it. Every bundle Ruby kept has a byte-identical bundle at the same position in the port's sequence (the final one included), and the stylesheet entries are identical, so the only difference is the port's two intermediate bundles that Ruby's wall clock overwrote. Another production recording of this step, in which no two of its writes shared a second, kept all 15 entries, each byte-identical to the port's. No fixed clock reproduces Ruby's collisions; the pins below hold the exact entries of the committed recording.",
